<section id="subscribing">
  <h2>Subscribing to events</h2>
  <p>
    Register a webhook with <code>POST /webhooks</code>. The request requires a <code>url</code> and an
    <code>events</code> array whose values must come from the ten spec-declared event names listed above.
    An optional <code>secret</code> enables HMAC payload verification, and <code>active</code>
    (default <code>true</code>) controls whether the subscription is live. A successful registration
    returns <code>201 Created</code>. Delete a subscription with <code>DELETE /webhooks/{webhookId}</code>,
    which returns <code>204 No Content</code>.
  </p>
  <div class="note warn">
    <strong>Undocumented events cannot be subscribed to.</strong> <code>task.due_date_approaching</code> and
    <code>task.overdue</code> are emitted by the platform but are not part of the <code>events</code> enum in the
    specification, so they cannot be requested at registration time. Existing endpoints observed receiving them
    (such as webhook <code>301</code> in the sample) appear to receive all events regardless of subscription.
    If your integration depends on these events, verify actual delivery behavior against your own endpoint and
    track the spec update in the discrepancy report before relying on them.
  </div>
</section>

<section id="delivery">
  <h2>Delivery and retry semantics</h2>
  <p>
    TaskFlow delivers events by HTTP <code>POST</code> to the destination URL registered for the webhook.
    A delivery is considered successful when the endpoint responds with an HTTP 2xx status.
    When the endpoint returns an error response, TaskFlow retries the delivery — the production sample shows
    <strong>3 retry attempts</strong> for the failed <code>project.deleted</code> delivery
    (<code>d-90008</code>, destination returned <code>502</code>) — and if all attempts fail, the delivery is
    marked <code>final_status: "failed"</code> and is not re-attempted further.
  </p>
  <p>Recommendations for consumer endpoints:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Respond quickly with <code>2xx</code> and process the payload asynchronously; slow handlers risk timeouts.</li>
    <li>Treat event delivery as <em>at-least-once</em>: deduplicate using <code>task_id</code>/<code>project_id</code> plus the event type and timestamp.</li>
    <li>Do not rely on ordering between events; in the sample, related events (<code>task.updated</code> and <code>task.assigned</code>) can both fire for one user action.</li>
    <li>Monitor for missed <code>project.deleted</code>-style events by periodically reconciling state, since permanently failed deliveries are not re-sent.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section id="security">
  <h2>Verifying payloads (HMAC)</h2>
  <p>
    When a webhook is registered with a <code>secret</code>, TaskFlow signs each payload with that shared secret
    using HMAC. Verify the signature server-side before trusting the payload contents. Keep the secret out of
    client-side code and rotate it by re-registering the webhook with a new <code>secret</code> value.
  </p>
</section>

